---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout title="Church Launch Kit">
  <article class="launch-kit">
    <section class="page-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">
        For parents, mentors, and church leaders · Sample materials
      </span>
      <h1>Church launch kit</h1>
      <p>
        A simple way to try Foundation Framework with your church. Adapt these
        prototype materials with your leaders before use.
      </p>
      <button data-print>Print launch kit</button>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>A first meeting · About 45 minutes</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Introduce the purpose · 5 minutes.</strong> Explain the four
          pillars and the goal of faithful responsibility, service, and teaching
          others.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Explore together · 10 minutes.</strong> Open an Apprentice
          challenge. Read its purpose, Scripture, requirements, and
          demonstration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Choose a starting point · 10 minutes.</strong> Offer
          Foundation 7 as an optional path. Let participants choose a manageable
          challenge from any pillar.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Arrange help · 10 minutes.</strong> Match the task with an
          appropriate parent, teacher, mentor, or skilled member. Agree on
          supervision and supplies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Agree on a follow-up · 10 minutes.</strong> Set a time to
          discuss practice and observe the demonstration. Explain how the church
          will keep approved records.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>An introduction for parents</h2>
      <p>
        Foundation Framework helps participants grow in biblical understanding
        and practical responsibility with adults in their local church.
        Apprentice challenges build broad experience across Identity,
        Stewardship, Service, and Dominion.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your role is to help choose realistic tasks, arrange adult instruction,
        encourage follow-through, and discuss what was learned. Foundation 7
        offers a starting point, but participants may begin anywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        The website’s progress stays on one device. It records checklist work
        and readiness, not official approval. Printed sheets can support
        conversations and your church’s own record keeping.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>A mentor’s meeting guide</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Read the participant’s challenge and adult guide before meeting.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ask the participant to explain the purpose and the help they need.
        </li>
        <li>
          Model unfamiliar steps, then give space for supervised practice.
        </li>
        <li>
          Observe the final demonstration using the listed readiness criteria.
        </li>
        <li>
          Give specific encouragement and one clear improvement if needed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ask how the skill can serve another person and, later, be passed on.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Follow your church’s arrangements for parent involvement, supervision,
        and authorized sign-off. Confirm approval in your church’s records
        rather than treating a checked box as evidence.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Before your church begins</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Review sample curriculum and select appropriate challenges.</li>
        <li>Decide who may instruct and who may approve each kind of work.</li>
        <li>Explain how participants can ask for help.</li>
        <li>
          Choose a simple paper or existing church record for approved
          completion.
        </li>
        <li>
          Arrange a follow-up conversation rather than relying on automated
          reminders.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <a href={`${base}completion-record/`}>
        Open a printable participant progress record
      </a>
    </section>
  </article>
  <script>
    document
      .querySelector("[data-print]")
      ?.addEventListener("click", () => window.print());
  </script>
</Layout>
